A||N||4||FieroSTETZ||found out something about my lifters||||1||
Z||000000||FieroSTETZ||03-01-2001||04:57 PM||||keep in mind I don't know much about lifters<P>a while back, i was driving my car and noticed a loud clanking sound coming from my engine and began asking questions about what it was... got lots of opinions, everyone prety much said rebuild or get a new engine- i decided to tear off my valve covers and see whats up. well, don't see anything so i keep tearing, and finally i get some of my lifters out. well, i think "what if its a plugged lifter? would it do that?" so i take the lifter over to my air compressor and blow 80 psi of air through it. wow, nothing happens. eventually, i discover that about 3/4 of my lifters will not let me blow air through them. i am on my way to autozone to buy lifters and pushrods, could the lifters have caused the loud clanking, or am i wasting my time with this? thanks much!||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||03-01-2001||07:50 PM||||If you drop new lifters on an old cam niether one may last long. Something to keep in mind if you have more trouble later. It's one of those hard to predict kind of things.||||reg||1
Z||000002||gabriel||03-01-2001||08:41 PM||||&gt;If you drop new lifters on an old cam niether one may last long. Something to keep in mind if you have more trouble later. It's one of those hard to predict kind of things.<P>  Have to agree.  The lifters will show a wear pattern specific to that cam lobe.<P>  Replacing just lifters is usually a temporary fix - after all the valvetrain is wearing at pretty much the same rate. - Cams, cam bearings, valves, lifters, seals, etc. If one component is going the others usually aren't too far behind & I always replaced/refurbished them as a system.<P>   But you could have luck here & have no problems for quite while.  <P>   It's your (judgement) call.<P>  Good luck. -g||||reg||1
Z||000003||FieroSTETZ||03-02-2001||03:30 AM||||well, the main point of me putting in new lifters is to get a few thousand more miles out of it while i am building my new engine for it- this is my daily transportation right now. also, i installed the new lifters and pushrods, and crank the engine iver, but no oil comes through the pushrods, or before pushrod installation, not oil came through the hole in the side of the lifter bore ole (lifter was not in yet) also, the oil in this car had always been mobil 1 (except since the knocking started, changed the oil rwice a week when it started) and i think that contributed to the fact that there is NO wear on the old lifters at all (not visibly at least. everything inside the engine is in awesome condition, except for a buncha plugged lifters||||reg||1
Z||000004||StuGood||03-02-2001||03:29 PM||||Not trying to start anything here. Just some data points...<P>I have had success replacing lifters alone (without replacing the cam), where the cam showed no obvious wear.  Ran perfectly fine afterwards, quite a few years later in some cases.  As Ogre suggests, I may have just been lucky.  What I'm saying is, I would risk it.  JMHO.<P>I imagine that any of the lifters that allowed you to blow air through them particularly freely, might have been leaking down and needed to be replaced.  Could have been the source of your original problem (the noise), too.  So, I'd have done the same thing.<P>As far as "pre-filling" lifters goes, and checking the oiling situation, here's what Stu do:  After you install the lifters, pushrods, etc., run the oil pump through the distributor hole.  Use a (5/16"-?)hex socket on a long extension chucked into an electric drill to drive the oil pump shaft.  When you feel like the lifters are filled with oil and everything is oiling properly, finish assembling the engine and start it up.<P>Do I always do it this way?  No - I've done it twice, both times on somebody else's car - mostly to make them feel good about it   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.  <P>It doesn't hurt anything - sometimes makes it trickier to reinstall the distributor.  No matter.  It's their engine, and if it makes them happy, I'm happy   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">    <BR>       <p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 03-02-2001).]||||reg||1
